  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B044",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B050",
    "B010"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I move optical lattice clocks ahead of my opening preference after considering the unusually coherent case for both attribution and maturity. Katori's magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept and Ye's decisive experimental development form a readily stated, complementary achievement. The clocks are independently reproduced precision instruments, not merely an anticipated capability, and their uses in fundamental tests and geodesy demonstrate a durable new observational tool.",
    "case_against": "The strongest reservation is that this can look like exceptional metrological technology rather than a discrete discovery, and that its citation must distinguish the lattice-clock invention from the wider history of optical frequency metrology and later performance refinements.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that lattice clocks are a mature, broadly enabling measurement platform, while stressing the need to separate the original invention from broader optical-clock development.",
        "response": "I agree with that delimitation. It is precisely the magic-wavelength lattice-clock architecture, rather than precision timekeeping in general, that gives this proposal a crisp scientific core and manageable attribution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson places the EHT black-hole images first because images of both M87* and Sgr A* make the observational result mature, though he notes difficult credit allocation in the large collaboration.",
        "response": "The EHT achievement remains an exceptionally strong alternative, but the collaboration-scale attribution problem is material. For this round, the two-person lattice-clock case offers a more sharply delimited contribution without diminishing the scientific stature of black-hole imaging."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson emphasizes that aberration-corrected electron optics has been broadly deployed for decades and transformed atomic-scale materials measurement, while calling for historical scrutiny of credit.",
        "response": "That is a persuasive instrument-builder's case and B010 remains close behind. I give B044 the present edge because its conceptual invention and independent experimental realization are more compactly represented by the named pair."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether additional early contributors to lattice-clock development have claims that would alter the final attribution boundary.",
      "Whether the committee judges the demonstrated precision platform sufficiently distinct from broader optical-frequency metrology."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
